L000346	\section{A Brief Reminder of the AdS$_3$ QSC}\label{SM:AdS3QSC}
L000347	In this appendix, we recap the general formulas for the AdS$_3$ QSC and give details on the algorithm for the perturbative solution for the particular states considered in this letter.
L000348	
L000349	The AdS$_3$ QSC consists of two $\algpsu(1,1|2)$ Q-systems connected through analytic continuation. Each Q-system consists of 16 Q-functions satisfying QQ-relations \begin{align}
L000350	    Q_{a|i}^+-Q_{a|i}^-&=\bP_a\bQ_i, & \bQ_i&=-\bP^a Q_{a|i}^+,\\
L000351	    Q_{\dot{a}|\dot{k}}^+-Q_{\dot{a}|\dot{k}}^-&=\bP_{\dot{a}}\bQ_{\dot{k}}, & \bQ_{\dot{k}}&=-\bP^{\dot{a}} Q_{\dot{a}|\dot{k}}^+,
L000352	\end{align}
L000353	where $f^{\pm}=f(u\pm\frac{i}{2})$. These functions have the asymptotics
L000354	\begin{equation}\label{eq:AsymptoticsQSM}
L000355	    \bP_{a} \simeq \mathbb{A}_{a} u^{M_{a}}\,,
L000356	    \quad
L000357	    \bP^{a} \simeq \mathbb{A}^{a} u^{-M_{a}-1}\,,
L000358	    \quad
L000359	    \bQ_{k} \simeq \mathbb{B}_{k} u^{\hat{M}_{k}}\,,
L000360	    \quad
L000361	    \bQ^{k} \simeq \mathbb{B}^{k} u^{-\hat{M}_{k}-1},
L000362	\end{equation}
L000363	\begin{align}
L000364	    M_{a} &= \frac{1}{2}\{-J+K-2,J-K\} - \frac{\hat{B}}{2}\,,
L000365	    &
L000366	    \hat{M}_{k} &= \frac{1}{2}\{\Delta-S,-\Delta+S-2\} + \frac{\hat{B}}{2}\\
L000367	    M_{\dot{a}} &= \frac{1}{2}\{-J-K,J+K-2\}-\frac{\check{B}}{2}\,,
L000368	    &
L000369	    \hat{M}_{\dot{k}} &= \frac{1}{2}\left\{\Delta+S-2,-\Delta-S\right\}+\frac{\check{B}}{2}\,,
L000370	\end{align}
L000371	and the leading coefficients $\mathbb{A}_a$ are given as
L000372	\begin{align}
L000373	    \mathbb{A}_1\mathbb{A}^{1} &= - \mathbb{A}_2 \mathbb{A}^{2} =  -\frac{\ii}{4}\frac{(\Delta-J+K-S)(\Delta+J-K-S+2)}{J-K+1},\\
L000374	    \mathbb{A}_{\dot{1}}\mathbb{A}^{\dot1} &= - \mathbb{A}_{\dot2} \mathbb{A}^{\dot 2} =  -\frac{\ii}{4}\frac{(\Delta-J-K+S)(\Delta+J+K+S-2)}{J+K-1},\\
L000375	    \mathbb{B}_1\mathbb{B}^{1} &= - \mathbb{B}_2 \mathbb{B}^{2} =  \frac{\ii}{4}\frac{(\Delta-J+K-S)(\Delta+J-K-S+2)}{\Delta-S+1},\\
L000376	    \mathbb{B}_{\dot{1}}\mathbb{B}^{\dot1} &= - \mathbb{B}_{\dot2} \mathbb{B}^{\dot 2} =  \frac{\ii}{4}\frac{(\Delta-J-K+S)(\Delta+J+K+S-2)}{\Delta+S-1}.
L000377	\end{align} (Following \cite{Cavaglia:2022xld}, we changed $S\rightarrow-S$ and $K\rightarrow-K$ compared to \cite{Ekhammar:2024kzp}.)
L000378	The $\bQ$-functions can be found from the $\bP$-functions by solving the Baxter equation \begin{align}
L000379	    D_1^- \bQ_k^{++}-D_2\bQ_k+D_1^+\bQ_k^{--}=0,
L000380	\end{align} where \begin{align*}
L000381	    D_1&=\epsilon_{ab}(\bP^a)^-(\bP^b)^+,\\
L000382	    D_2&=\epsilon_{ab}(\bP^a)^{--}(\bP^b)^{++}-\bP_c(\bP^c)^{--}\epsilon_{ab}\bP^a(\bP^b)^{++}.
L000383	\end{align*}
L000384	As a boundary condition on the Q-system we have the constraint
L000385	\begin{equation}\label{eq:detConstraint}
L000386	    \det(Q_{a|i})=\det(Q_{\dot a|\dot i})=1.
L000387	\end{equation}
L000388	On the first Riemann sheet the $\bP$-functions have a short branch cut on the interval $[-2h,2h]$. By consistency, the $\bQ$-functions have on the first Riemann sheet an infinite tower of cuts, with the first one on the real line interval $[-2h,2h]$ and the rest of the tower spaced by $\ii$ and extending into the lower half plane. The $Q_{a|i}$ functions have a similar tower of cuts but shifted by $-\frac{\ii}{2}$ into the lower half-plane.
L000389	
L000390	In this letter, we consider states with $K=0$ and $\Delta(h=0)=J+S$. For these states, we find $\bP_a=\epsilon_{ab}\bP^b$ and the parity relation \begin{align}
L000391	    \bP_a(-u)&=\mathbf{g}_a{}^b\bP_b(u),
L000392	\end{align} with $\mathbf{g}_{a}{}^{ b}=(-1)^{M_a}\delta_a^b$ and similar for the dotted functions. The analytic continuation of the $\bP$-functions through their first branch cut on a path $\overline{\gamma}$ counter-clockwise around the point $u=-2h$ can then be written in the form \begin{align}
L000393	    \bP_a=\mu^R_a{}^{\dot b}(\bP_{\dot{b}})^{\overline{\gamma}}.
L000394	\end{align} The matrix $\mu^R$ is constructed as \begin{align}\label{eq:muReg}
L000395	    \mu^R_a{}^{\dot b}=-Q_{a|k}(u+\frac{\ii}{2})\epsilon^{kl}G_{l}{}^{\dot m}e^{-\ii\pi\hat{M}_{\dot{m}}}Q_{\dot c|\dot m}\epsilon^{\dot c\dot d}\mathbf{g}_{\dot d}{}^{\dot b},
L000396	\end{align} in terms of the gluing matrix \eqref{eq:gluing} and it has no branch cuts on the real line. An analogous equation gives $\mu^R_{\dot a}{}^b$.
L000397	
L000398	
L000399	\section{Analytic solutions of the QSC}
L000400	
L000401	The QSC can be solved analytically using the coupling $h$ as a small expansion parameter. The algorithm presented here is a refinement of the algorithm developed in \cite{Cavaglia:2022xld}.
L000402	
L000403	For $J=2,3$ the tree-level ($h=0$) $\bP$-functions are monomials in $u$. Using this, it is easy to solve the Baxter equation for $\bQ$ and the QQ-relations for $Q_{a|i}$, followed by applying the determinant constraint to fully fix the tree-level solution.
L000404	
L000405	At the QQ-scale we make the Ansatz \begin{equation}\label{eq:QQscaleAnsatz}
L000406	    \bP_a=\mathbb{A}_a u^{M_a}\left(1+\sum_{n=1}^\infty c_{a,n}\frac{h^{2n}}{u^{2n}}\right),
L000407	\end{equation} with the coefficients expanding as $c_{a,n}=c_{a,n}^{(0)}+\mathcal{O}(h)$. We use this Ansatz to solve order by order the QQ-relations for the $Q_{a|i}$- and $\bQ$-functions. The asymptotics of the Q-functions and the determinant constraint \eqref{eq:detConstraint} fix most of the integration constants. Using \eqref{eq:muReg} we can then construct $\mu^R(u),\dot{\mu}^R(u)$ with the parameter of the gluing matrix expanding as $\alpha=\alpha^{(0)}h^J+\mathcal{O}(h^{J+1})$. Since these $\mu^R$ matrices do not have a branch cut on the real line, we can change variables to $v=\frac{u}{h}$ and at finite order in $h$ the $\mu^R$ are a finite order polynomial in $v$. Requiring $\mu^R$ to be unambiguous, \textit{viz.} the absence of any regularisation parameter, provides at this stage some additional constraints on the integration constants. We can then compute the matrix $W_a{}^c(v)=\mu^R_a{}^{\dot{b}}\mu_{\dot b}^R{}^c$ which describes the double analytic continuation of the $\bP$-functions \begin{equation}
L000408	    \bP_a=W_a{}^b\bP_b^{2\overline{\gamma}}.
L000409	\end{equation} By construction $W$ has unit determinant. As the $\bP$-functions should be regular near the branch points $u=\pm 2h$, the trace of $W$ is constrained as \begin{align}
L000410	    \textrm{tr}\,W(v=\pm2)=2,
L000411	\end{align} such that $W$ approaches the identity matrix near the branch points of $\bP$.
L000412	
L000413	The final constraint in order to fix the solution of the QSC is the analytic continuation. This cannot be imposed using the Ansatz \eqref{eq:QQscaleAnsatz} in the variable $u$. Instead, we make a second Ansatz \eqref{eq:WeakExpansion} using the Zhukovsky variable $x$ \begin{equation}
L000414	    \bP_{a} = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\sum_{m=0}^{k} h^kl^{m}\bP^{(k,m)}_{a},
L000415	\end{equation}
L000416	with $l=\frac{\ii}{2\pi}\log\left(\frac{x-1}{x+1}\right)$ and $\bP_{a}^{(k,m)}$ are Laurent polynomials in $x$ of finite degree. Applying the definition of the Zhukovsky variable and expanding the new Ansatz at small $h$ we can relate the coefficients in both Ans{\"a}tze. Noting that $v=x+\frac{1}{x}$, $x^{\overline{\gamma}}=\frac{1}{x}$ and $l^{\overline{\gamma}}=l+\frac{1}{2}$, we finally test the analytic continuation \begin{equation}
L000417	    \bP_a=\mu^R_a{}^{\dot b}(\bP_{\dot{b}})^{\overline{\gamma}}.
L000418	\end{equation}
L000419	Following this algorithm to an appropriate order in $h$, the dimension of the state in consideration is fixed to the desired order.
L000420	
L000421	
L000422	\section{Comparison with the AdS$_3$ Virasoro-Shapiro Amplitude}\label{sec:comp-vs}
L000423	
L000424	The states we study differ from those appearing in the Virasoro-Shapiro
L000425	amplitude approach~\cite{Chester:2024wnb,Jiang:2025oar,Jiang:2026xnh}. Following the AdS$_3$
L000426	integrability convention~\cite{Borsato:2013qpa}, we label multiplets by
L000427	the highest weight state obtained from the superconformal primary by
L000428	acting with two right-moving supercharges. Our states have
L000429	even spin $S\in2\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ and equal left and right R-charges
L000430	$\frac{J}{2}$, so the corresponding superconformal primaries have
L000431	odd spin $S-1$ and R-charges $[\frac{J}{2},\frac{J-2}{2}]$. The
L000432	Virasoro-Shapiro amplitude instead exchanges multiplets whose
L000433	superconformal primaries have spin $l\in2\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ and equal
L000434	left and right R-charges $\frac{p-1}{2}$ ($p\in\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$).
L000435	
L000436	
L000437	
L000438	
L000439	
L000440	
L000441	We note in particular that for $J=2$, the $(4,4)$ superconformal
L000442	primary is \textit{not} part of the lowest massive string multiplet. In contrast, in
L000443	$\mathcal{N}=4$ SYM theory and its string dual, the analogous state (meaning the $\mathcal{N}=4$
L000444	state with all Cartan charges outside $\mathfrak{psu}(1,1|2)^{\oplus 2}$
L000445	set to zero) would be a descendant of the Konishi multiplet, obtained
L000446	by the action of two supercharges. These supercharges, however, are not part the small $(4,4)$ superconformal algebra and are broken in the AdS$_3$ setting. As a result, the $J=2$
L000447	$(4,4)$ primary we study here sits in a distinct long multiplet
L000448	within the lowest massive string level, unrelated to the Konishi.
